WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.080
Hey Thoughty2 here.

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:05.680
You don’t need to leave our planet to find
people who you can’t understand.

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:09.460
For some people, you don’t even need to
leave your own house; you’re still trying

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:14.519
to understand why your mum has suddenly outlawed
gluten and why your dad thinks video games

00:00:14.519 --> 00:00:18.160
are the devil’s work but spent his whole
life playing with a model train set.

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:22.710
But, with some pointing and shouting at them,
you can at least manage to communicate on

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:23.710
a basic level.

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:28.960
But what about if we really came into contact
with an alien species; is it realistic to

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:36.690
think we could communicate and what form would
that take; speaking, writing, emojis?

00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:41.010
We can look to the early explorers of our
planet, seeing how cultures collided, when

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:42.550
they met for the first time.

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:47.490
But at least they were of the same species,
things might not be so easy when you’re

00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:51.800
trying to communicate with a creature that
has nipples for eyes and eyes where its nipples

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:56.960
should be.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:01.300
Obviously our first problem is actually finding
some aliens to talk to us because it’s a

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:06.100
big old universe out there and I’m not sure
that Earth has made it into the galactic guidebook

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:08.620
yet, despite Ford Prefect’s best efforts.

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:15.180
It’s often said that the opening ceremony
to the 1936 Olympics was the first message

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:18.760
broadcast into space and there is some truth
to it.

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:23.400
It was at a high enough frequency that it
may have escaped the ionosphere and headed

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:24.960
off out the solar system.

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:30.350
But the likelihood that anyone would be able
to pick it up would be incredibly small because

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:37.000
it doesn’t have the power and concentrated
direction to get noticed, it would be lost

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:38.730
in the noise of the universe.

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:43.810
So thankfully, Hitler won’t be the first
face of mankind that the aliens see, you could

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:46.750
say, they will Nazi him coming.

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:52.040
We have made many intentional attempts both
to send messages and to listen out for them.

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:57.750
In August 1924, Mars was going to be closer
to Earth than at any point in the past century.

00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:02.810
So, the US decided to have a good listen and
declared National Radio Silence Day where

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:07.590
all radios were silent for five minutes on
the hour, every hour.

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:09.800
They used an airship to lift a radio receiver
3 kilometres off the ground.

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:10.800
In 1962, the Soviets used the Pluton complex
in Ukraine to broadcast a Morse code message

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:11.800
towards Venus.

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:12.800
It was just three words; MIR, LENIN, SSSR.

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:13.800
‘Mir’ means both peace and world and SSSR
is the Russian for USSR.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:14.800
Lenin was a good choice at the time but will
be unavailable to comment if the Venusians

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:15.800
do arrive.

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:18.090
So, let’s say we do get a message back,
it’s likely to be in the form of radio waves.

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:19.650
What do we do then?

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:22.670
The first thing is to try work out what each
part relates to.

00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:26.450
If it’s mathematics then hopefully we will
know what to look out for; things like the

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:33.800
universal constants such as Pi, e, numbers
that occur throughout the universe.

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:37.730
If it’s language then it becomes much more
problematic since we don’t have some kind

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:40.620
of Rosetta Stone.

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:46.660
The Rosetta Stone was a carved rock found
in the town of Rosetta, Egypt, in 1799.

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:52.200
It was of incredible importance to our understanding
of ancient Egyptian hieroglyphs since it had

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:57.020
a trilingual inscription; hieroglyphs, Egyptian
script and Greek.

00:02:57.020 --> 00:03:01.550
And this highlights our problem; without the
comparison to base our work off, it can be

00:03:01.550 --> 00:03:05.750
impossible to ever make a translation of a
new language.

00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:10.459
We don’t even need alien languages to test
this theory; there are plenty of untranslated

00:03:10.459 --> 00:03:12.930
texts here on earth.

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:18.970
The Liber Linteus is a manuscript from around
250 BC, that was found wrapped around a mummy.

00:03:18.970 --> 00:03:24.450
It’s one of the few examples of the Etruscan
language, from ancient Italy, and we’ve

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:26.840
barely managed to suss any of it out.

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:30.629
There’s also a language called Linear A,
from ancient Greek, that we’re struggling

00:03:30.629 --> 00:03:34.720
with, despite it sounding like a Microsoft
Typeface from the 90s.

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:40.380
And there’s Proto-Elamite, used for a brief
200-year window in ancient Iran - we’ve

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:45.300
still no idea what they were up to and what
secrets these scripts hold.

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:50.320
Some linguists have thought that perhaps letting
language evolve naturally, with all it’s

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:55.450
weird quirks and local idiosyncrasies, is
not the best way to go about things.

00:03:55.450 --> 00:03:59.610
English is taking over as the international
language, thanks largely to its dominance

00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:05.450
over the internet, but attempts have been
made to create a universal language; which

00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:09.550
would hopefully make life easier when aliens
join the action.

00:04:09.550 --> 00:04:15.720
Kenneth Searight created Sona in 1935, in
response to some of the new constructed languages,

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:19.379
or conlangs as they are known, that were appearing;
like Esperanto.

00:04:19.379 --> 00:04:24.699
His issue was that these new conlangs were
all very European focussed, which was not

00:04:24.699 --> 00:04:26.400
truly global.

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:28.449
Sona was also agglutinative.

00:04:28.449 --> 00:04:32.770
This basically means that when you connect
parts together, you don’t change the form

00:04:32.770 --> 00:04:38.860
or sound or spelling – which is what most
languages do and all this fusing things together

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:41.520
makes them much more complicated.

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:48.210
Charles K Bliss, a Jewish concentration camp
survivor, tried to create a written pictogram

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:53.430
language, one that would be universal and
one that evil politicians could not manipulate,

00:04:53.430 --> 00:04:56.530
leading to the horrors that he’d witnessed.

00:04:56.530 --> 00:05:00.770
Although Blissymbols never spread around the
world, their logical structure was found to

00:05:00.770 --> 00:05:04.379
be very useful in teaching disabled children
to communicate.

00:05:04.379 --> 00:05:09.529
But we don’t know whether we will be speaking
or listening first when it comes to alien

00:05:09.529 --> 00:05:10.529
communication.

00:05:10.529 --> 00:05:16.039
So, in order to have our ears prepared for
alien arrival, Dr John Elliott of Leeds Metropolitan

00:05:16.039 --> 00:05:20.949
University in England has designed a piece
of software that he hopes will be able to

00:05:20.949 --> 00:05:27.789
tell what is and isn’t a language and, if
it is, what patterns it contains.

00:05:27.789 --> 00:05:31.759
Finding out what form the message is in will
be the key.

00:05:31.759 --> 00:05:37.300
If you get a jpeg from the stars but you think
it’s music, it’s going to be difficult

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:41.550
to decipher the message, although you will
probably make a fortune as a pioneering new

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:42.689
techno artist.

00:05:42.689 --> 00:05:45.080
You can just see hipsters lapping that up.

00:05:45.080 --> 00:05:49.559
The voyager probes, that we sent out into
the depths of our solar system, both carry

00:05:49.559 --> 00:05:51.812
a gold disc which plays like a record.

00:05:51.812 --> 00:05:52.812
The back cover dedicates a lot of space to
explaining how to play the record and how

00:05:52.812 --> 00:05:55.460
to extract the information; since there is
music, sounds and images.

00:05:55.460 --> 00:06:00.399
We used pictures of hydrogen atoms to define
the time scale since they are the most common

00:06:00.399 --> 00:06:03.770
element and their state change is a universal
constant.

00:06:03.770 --> 00:06:06.750
There was also a diagram of how to play the
record.

00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:11.419
But, given the fact that half a page of Ikea
instruction drives most of us to madness,

00:06:11.419 --> 00:06:26.270
I’m not holding out too much hope for 

00:06:26.270 --> 00:06:28.500
the aliens.

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:34.469
Thanks for watching.

